#
# FREE CHAIN ENDS. The three hydrogens of an N-terminal ammonium and the
# second oxygen of a C-terminal carboxylate exist in AMBER for all 20
# residues (templates NALA..NVAL, CALA..CVAL) and had no rule here at all,
# so pdb2spn dropped them WITHOUT LOGGING IT: 071's 377-atom input built a
# 374-particle network, and the .rbody's _NTER/_CTER groups and the
# .bi.ff's ammonium rotor and carboxylate improper had nothing to resolve.
#
# One type per atom even though the three ammonium hydrogens share a charge:
# two atoms of ONE residue sharing a type both resolve to the first, and the
# second then gets no spring at all.
#
# THESE FOUR RULES ARE NO LONGER WHAT PARAMETERISES A FREE CHAIN END. They
# could not be: a rule keyed on (residue, atom) alone leaves the atoms a
# terminal residue SHARES with the interior one at the interior charge, and
# AMBER retypes those too -- NALA's N is an ammonium N3 at +0.1414 e, not an
# amide N at -0.4157. That left a terminal residue at -1.2719 e where AMBER
# has +1, and +1.0000 e where AMBER has -1. The qualified rules at the end of
# this file fix it; measured on 071, every one of its 377 atoms now carries
# AMBER's own charge, against 29 atoms off by up to 0.5571 e before.
#
# They stay for the residue whose terminus is NOT declared: a chain BROKEN
# mid-chain is no free end, and this is where its OXT or its ammonium
# hydrogens are claimed. See the note at the end of this file.
#
AH1 ALA H1
AH2 ALA H2
AH3 ALA H3
AOXT ALA OXT
