#
# ======================================================================
# FREE CHAIN ENDS, PARAMETERISED FROM AMBER'S OWN TERMINAL TEMPLATES
# ======================================================================
#
# A residue at a free chain end is NALA/CALA, not ALA, and the retyping is
# neither cosmetic nor local: of NALA's 12 atoms, 9 of the 10 it shares with
# ALA carry a different charge -- the nitrogen goes from -0.4157 to +0.1414 e
# as it becomes an ammonium N3, but so does CB, -0.1825 to -0.0597 e, three
# bonds away. Over the 20 residues and both ends, 628 shared atoms are retyped
# and 79 exist only at a terminus. What it buys is the net charge: exactly
# +1 e at an N-terminus and -1 e at a C-terminus, against -1.2719 e and
# +1.0000 e from the interior parameters.
#
# The residue column carries ":NTER" or ":CTER", the same qualifier the .bi.ff
# carries on the same column, read by the same code -- see
# src/topology/TerminalPosition.hpp. A residue takes the rules matching its own
# position INSTEAD of the unqualified ones, so every atom of a terminal residue
# needs a rule here, not only the ones whose charge changed; an atom no rule
# claims is dropped from the network, and reported per atom.
#
# THE GRAIN NAME IS THE INTERIOR ONE ON PURPOSE. A terminal alanine's nitrogen
# is still "AN", because that name is the particle's identity for every file
# keyed on it: ProteinDonorAcceptor.hbond, ProteinPeptideSite.psite, and the
# reverse lookup that resolves a .rbody or .bi.ff atom name onto a renamed
# particle. Renaming the terminal grains would drop every terminal residue out
# of all three, silently. What the position changes is the PARAMETERS, so the
# qualifier goes on the force-field type instead: "AN:NTER" in amber.nbi.ff
# holds the ammonium charge where "AN" holds the amide's. See
# ReduceRule::forcefield_key.
#
# A FREE TERMINUS IS NOT THE SAME AS A CHAIN END. A structure carved out of a
# larger one has a first residue whose nitrogen is still an amide with its one
# hydrogen, and a last residue whose carbonyl is still a carbonyl; these rules
# must not apply there. Measured on the examples: 056's lone methionine would
# come out at +0.60 e instead of 0, and 4 of 077's chain ends with it. So a
# free terminus is recognised the way AMBER and OpenMM recognise it, from the
# residue's own atoms -- the ammonium hydrogens for the N end, OXT for the C
# end. That is also why the interior H1/H2/H3 and OXT rules above stay: a chain
# BROKEN mid-chain is no terminus either, and those atoms are claimed there.
#
# The first ammonium hydrogen is spelled H as often as H1 by OpenMM, and the
# amide "H" rule is NOT in the N-terminal set, so each N-terminal H1 type takes
# a second rule for that spelling -- without it the atom would be claimed by no
# rule at all and dropped.
#
# Generated from amber99sb.xml (HIS as HID, as above): one rule per atom of
# each NXXX/CXXX template, named after the interior rule for the same atom.
# See the matching section of amber.nbi.ff for the parameters.
#
AN ALA:NTER N
AH1 ALA:NTER H1
AH1 ALA:NTER H
AH2 ALA:NTER H2
AH3 ALA:NTER H3
ACA ALA:NTER CA
AHA ALA:NTER HA
ACB ALA:NTER CB
